@h1 Design principles h1@

These are the ideas that shape every decision in Wit. Read them before proposing
a change or building on the AST — most questions about why Wit is the way it is
are answered here.

@h2 Prose comes first h2@

Structure is opt-in. The constraint that governs the whole language is that the
source stays readable as prose: plain sentences render as plain paragraphs, and a
writer never has to leave the writing to satisfy the format.

@h2 Content in bodies, parameters are metadata h2@

A node's content goes in its @strong body strong@; anything that merely describes
the node — a link's address, an image's size — rides in @strong parameters strong@.
This one rule keeps documents clean as they grow, and it is elevated from an
authoring tip to a project value.

@h2 A small reserved core h2@

Wit ships a small core vocabulary that mirrors HTML's semantic shapes — headings,
lists, links, images, tables, sections. Renderers commit to handling these
faithfully. Everything beyond the core is either a writer's own definition
(written @@@code #name code@@@) or the opaque pass-through
(@@@code @node code@@@), which carries parameters straight to the renderer so it
can dispatch on a @code type code@ convention.

@@@pre
#highlight ||content||
@node(type highlight) ::content:: node@
highlight#

@highlight Some text highlight@
pre@@@

The core stays small on purpose. If a definition can express something, it does
not become a core node.

@h2 Not Turing-complete h2@

Wit is not a programming language. The script bridge, @code <% %> code@, is the
single explicit escape hatch for general computation — everything else is
declarative. That boundary is deliberate: it keeps documents analysable and
their output deterministic.

@h2 A file format, not a system h2@

Wit is not a CMS, an editor, or a general templating engine. It is a
@strong file format strong@ — a way to write a document that many different
programs can then consume. It is not an inline rich-text editor language, and it
does not do arbitrary string interpolation in prose.

@h2 Engineering constraints as principles h2@

The implementation constraints are principles too: zero runtime dependencies,
deterministic output, source locations that survive every stage, small files and
functions, and a corpus of fixtures that acts as the executable specification.
Tests come first — a behaviour is not real until a fixture pins it.

@h2 Common contributor mistakes h2@

@ul
@li @strong Reaching for a new core node. strong@ If a definition would do, add a
definition, not a reserved name. li@
@li @strong Adding interpolation to prose. strong@ String interpolation in ordinary
prose is a non-goal — data reaches prose through dotted access, not template
holes. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

